{
  "id": "wulfic-session17-period-detectors",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Two checks for a repeating key length",
  "question": "Do source Test8 known-text key inference and Test9 split-column/Kasiski checks identify a repeat period across their named LP2 pages?",
  "reader_question": "Do these pages show a repeating key length?",
  "summary": "The source known-text check produced no report over its 25% line. The split-column check flagged 29 of 34 pages, the same as the middle complete shuffled search.",
  "finding": "Test8 examined 4,239 distinct page/text/offset lag grids (8,478 sign-equivalent source settings) on LP2-04–17, with a best 10/76=13.2% key-lag match and no strict >25% reports. Twelve of 49 shuffled whole searches equaled or exceeded that best. Test9 flagged 29/34 LP2-04–37 pages; 26/49 whole shuffles flagged as many, and 5/49 matched its selected maximum ratio 2.28. Both source detectors registered the planted period13.",
  "limitations": "The source candidate text set and sampled offsets do not cover unknown plaintext. Test9 has a permissive 1.3× column/page line: it flagged 27/34 uniform planted-control pages. Its Kasiski prime factors have no source acceptance threshold. Forty-nine within-page shuffles preserve frequency, not an encryption model, and do not support a universal no-period claim.",
  "next_steps": "A period claim should state a page region, alphabet, clock and predicted held-out reading before inspecting a peak; compare the complete page/period search with controls matched to that model.",
  "outcome": "not-supported",
  "tags": [
